---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import { CirclePlay } from "lucide-react";
import agentsCapture from "../assets/agents.png";
import chatCapture from "../assets/chat.png";
import knowledgeCapture from "../assets/knowledge.png";
import loopsCapture from "../assets/loops.png";
import memoryCapture from "../assets/memory.png";
import triggersCapture from "../assets/triggers.png";
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { GITHUB_URL } from "../data/site";
import { CHAPTER_PARAM, tourChapters } from "../data/tour.mjs";
import { releaseFacts } from "../data/release";
import marketing from "../../marketing/screenshots.manifest.json";

/*
 * WHY SEVERAL CLAIMS ON THIS PAGE ARE NARROWER THAN THEY COULD BE.
 *
 * Every claim below was read against the RELEASED tag this site pins (core v0.2.7, apps
 * v0.2.2), and the ones that hold only in part say only the part that holds. Recorded here in
 * the frontmatter rather than beside the markup on purpose: an Astro template comment is
 * emitted into the HTML, and a retracted claim quoted verbatim in the shipped page is a string
 * a reader — or a scraper — can still find and believe (docs/site-audit.md, P12).
 *
 * SIX SECTIONS (#90). The page used to carry nine capability sections. The inbox and
 * notification rules fold into automation, the guardrails into goal loops and the artifacts
 * into chat, each as a line under "What you control" with no capture of its own; the Doctor
 * leaves /product. Four of their shots are retired in marketing/screenshots.manifest.json; the
 * inbox shot stays, placed on Home, which shows it.
 *
 * CUT ENTIRELY — the safety-profile bullet. `guardrails/policy.py` was a closed island when
 * this was first read. At v0.2.7 it is wired: the gateway and the composer's Execution Boundary
 * read `profile_for_session`. The bullet stays cut anyway, because /compare's `tool_approval`
 * row states what the profiles bound and what they do not, and a line here would carry the
 * claim without its limits.
 *
 * CHAT. The permission modes are Normal, Trust reads and Trust per chat; YOLO is gateway-wide,
 * auto-approves everywhere until it expires, and picking any other mode turns it off. The
 * memory mode (Persistent, Temporary, Incognito) is chosen when a chat is created and no route
 * changes it after. Regenerate and the variant switcher exist on the latest answer only, so the
 * page says "the latest answer". Branching needs a Persistent chat; rewind keeps the discarded
 * tail. ARTIFACTS: previews render for some types and office or video types do not, so the page
 * claims the library and its versions (the viewer's Versions rail, revert and compare), not
 * previews.
 *
 * GOAL LOOPS. Pause keeps the workers for Resume; Stop is final and keeps the findings, the
 * report and any unmerged work, so the page says "stop it for good" and what a stopped loop
 * keeps. Only an Attended loop stops to ask (an Unattended one never pauses), so the asking line
 * names the Attended loop.
 *
 * GUARDRAILS, narrowed. The incident kill switch holds every kind of unattended work at once
 * (clock and watch triggers, idle triggers and loop cycles, workflow runs, script-hook actions,
 * every subagent spawn, the inbound doors, the background maintenance) and resumes only on an
 * explicit confirm. It was said to "never affect interactive chat"; at v0.2.7 a chat's turns keep
 * running but a subagent a chat asks for is refused too, so the page says the turns keep running
 * and the spawns are held. Budgets are daily token and dollar ceilings on unattended spend only
 * (chat turns and Attended loops are not counted), unlimited until set: "on what your
 * automations spend". The budgets have no per-run dollar ceiling; an automation's own per-run
 * cost gate and a loop's cost cap are separate settings, not budgets. The prompt-scanning modes
 * are left off this page.
 *
 * MEMORY. Semantic and episodic recall, lessons from corrections, and promotion of repeated
 * episodes to facts all have live callers. The procedural layer is now read back at v0.2.7, but
 * only as a few global tool-outcome priors, so the page says "which tools have served it well"
 * rather than "recall" of everything it records. The memory page deletes facts, episodes, lessons
 * and entities and edits facts, so "edit" names facts alone.
 *
 * KNOWLEDGE. Entities are extracted only when a model is set, so the line says so. Knowledge is a
 * database and a folder of originals under the PersonalClaw home; no path is typed.
 *
 * AUTOMATION. The dashboard's create form offers an interval, a cron schedule, a one-shot, a
 * lifecycle event, a data event and a finished run; the other store kinds come through the
 * automation tools, so the page names the form's. An inbox row links back only when it carries a
 * reference (a channel message has none), so the page says "a row a loop, chat or run raised".
 *
 * WEBHOOKS. Both inbound doors (`inbound/webhook.py`) answer only programs on this machine and
 * refuse any other address before reading a token; a sender elsewhere comes in through a tunnel
 * or relay the owner runs here. The automation section says so and cites /compare#webhooks.
 *
 * AGENT RUNTIMES. The coding agents are apps over ACP; no count is typed and none is named, so a
 * catalogue change cannot leave this page wrong. Calls an agent asks about go through the host
 * gate (`acp/permission_authority.py`), and a call it ran without asking is recorded as ungated
 * (`acp/ungated.py`). Every cloud model provider is an app; core ships a local one and a small
 * offline fallback model in its own tree, so "no provider is built in" would be false.
 *
 * TELEMETRY. "No product telemetry" was wider than /compare's `telemetry` row: by default
 * PersonalClaw asks GitHub whether a newer release exists, at start and on an interval, and a
 * setting turns that off. The closing section says both halves and cites the row; no interval
 * is typed. The closing section no longer types a Python version (P4).
 *
 * WAITING ON YOU. Core's one list of everything waiting on you (`GET /api/waiting`) ships at
 * v0.2.7, and Home's Needs You reads it. The page still claims the inbox, which stays a
 * destination of its own, rather than that list.
 *
 * Every section here that talks about a row's subject cites that row, and
 * `npm run validate:compare` fails the build when one does not (scripts/product-claims.mjs).
 */

const chapters = tourChapters();

// Which release the tour shows, from its manifest entry: the note says so, and says the screens
// have moved when the recording is older than the release this site describes.
const tourEntry = marketing.videos.find((video) => video.id === "tour");
if (!tourEntry) throw new Error("product.astro: the marketing manifest has no tour entry");
// No version label in the hero (SITE-REDESIGN.md, hard rules): the note names the release by what it is.
const order = (a: string, b: string) => {
  const [x, y] = [a, b].map((v) => v.split(".").map(Number));
  for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) if (x[i] !== y[i]) return x[i] < y[i] ? -1 : 1;
  return 0;
};
const recorded = order(tourEntry.capturedAt, releaseFacts.core.version);
const tourNote =
  recorded === 0
    ? "Recorded at the release this site describes."
    : recorded < 0
      ? "It was recorded at an earlier release than this site describes, so some screens have changed since."
      : "It was recorded at a newer release than this site describes, so some screens may differ from it.";

/** `m:ss`, from the track's seconds. */
const clock = (seconds: number) =>
  `${Math.floor(seconds / 60)}:${String(Math.floor(seconds % 60)).padStart(2, "0")}`;

interface Capability {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  problem: string;
  capture: ImageMetadata;
  alt: string;
  controls: { text: string; cite?: { href: string; label: string } }[];
  docs: { href: string; label: string };
}

const capabilities: Capability[] = [
  {
    id: "chat",
    title: "Ask, then let it do the work.",
    problem:
      "A chat that can only answer leaves the doing to you. Here a conversation runs tools, writes files and starts longer work, and it stops to ask before a call you have not allowed.",
    capture: chatCapture,
    alt: "PersonalClaw chat with a conversation, its tool calls and the approval it waits on",
    controls: [
      { text: "Each chat's permission mode: Normal, Trust reads or Trust. YOLO approves everywhere until it runs out." },
      { text: "Its memory: Persistent, Temporary or Incognito, chosen when the chat starts." },
      { text: "Its model, chat by chat." },
      { text: "Where it goes next: branch from a message, rewind a turn, or regenerate the latest answer and switch between its versions." },
      { text: "The files it makes, kept in a library with every version, to revert or compare." }
    ],
    docs: { href: "/docs/architecture/chat-sessions", label: "How chat sessions work" }
  },
  {
    id: "loops",
    title: "Hand off a goal. Watch it work.",
    problem:
      "Long work does not fit in one reply. A goal loop plans, works cycle after cycle and reports against the goal you set, in a cockpit that shows its plan, its current cycle, its tool calls and what it produced.",
    capture: loopsCapture,
    alt: "A finished PersonalClaw goal loop with its cycle, time, spend, and the deliverable it reported",
    controls: [
      { text: "Pause and resume a loop, nudge it while it runs, or stop it for good. A stopped loop keeps its findings, its report and any work not yet merged." },
      { text: "Whether it asks: an Attended loop stops when it needs an answer, and the question waits in your inbox." },
      { text: "A kill switch that holds every kind of unattended work at once, subagent spawns included, until you resume it. Your chat turns keep running." },
      { text: "Daily token and dollar ceilings on what your automations spend." }
    ],
    docs: { href: "/docs/architecture/loops", label: "How goal loops work" }
  },
  {
    id: "memory",
    title: "Stop explaining yourself twice.",
    problem:
      "Every new chat with a model starts from nothing. Memory keeps what it learns about you: facts, past episodes, the lessons your corrections teach it, the facts it promotes from what keeps coming up, and which tools have served it well.",
    capture: memoryCapture,
    alt: "PersonalClaw memory view with layered learned context",
    controls: [
      { text: "What it holds: see all of it, edit its facts, and delete any fact, episode, lesson or entity." },
      { text: "Which chats it learns from: a Temporary chat neither reads nor writes it, and an Incognito chat reads it and writes nothing back." }
    ],
    docs: { href: "/docs/architecture/knowledge-memory#memory", label: "How memory works" }
  },
  {
    id: "knowledge",
    title: "Answers from your own sources.",
    problem:
      "A model knows the public web, not your notes. Knowledge holds what you add on purpose, kept apart from memory, in a database on your machine, and chat searches it when a question needs it.",
    capture: knowledgeCapture,
    alt: "PersonalClaw knowledge base with tagged source documents",
    controls: [
      { text: "What goes in: notes, links it fetches when you save them, and files from documents and spreadsheets to images, audio and video." },
      { text: "What it knows about each item: with a model set, the people, places and things in it." },
      { text: "Where it lives: one database and a folder of originals in your PersonalClaw home." }
    ],
    docs: { href: "/docs/architecture/knowledge-memory#knowledge", label: "How knowledge works" }
  },
  {
    id: "automation",
    title: "Work that starts without you asking.",
    problem:
      "Recurring work should not wait for you to remember it. Schedules, events and finished runs start work on their own, and what needs you lands in one inbox instead of a toast that scrolls past.",
    capture: triggersCapture,
    alt: "PersonalClaw automation triggers with schedules and status",
    controls: [
      { text: "When it runs: on an interval, a cron schedule or once, on a lifecycle or data event, or when another run finishes. A schedule can run an agent in the background." },
      {
        text: "Webhooks from programs on this machine only. A sender elsewhere comes in through a tunnel or relay you run here.",
        cite: { href: "/compare#webhooks", label: "What webhooks can and cannot do" }
      },
      { text: "On or off: every schedule and trigger has its own switch." },
      { text: "What interrupts you: Notify, Badge, Digest or Never, for each kind of notification." },
      { text: "One inbox for a loop's question, an approval, a proposed skill or a message. Each row links back to the loop, chat or run that raised it." }
    ],
    docs: { href: "/docs/architecture/tasks-triggers", label: "How tasks and triggers work" }
  },
  {
    id: "agents",
    title: "Bring the agent that fits the job.",
    problem:
      "One vendor's agent should not be the only one you can run. PersonalClaw runs its own agent, or a coding agent you install as an app over the Agent Client Protocol, and the models behind them are apps too.",
    capture: agentsCapture,
    alt: "PersonalClaw agents view showing configured agent runtimes",
    controls: [
      { text: "Which agent a chat runs on: the native agent, or a coding agent you installed." },
      { text: "One approval gate for every call an installed agent asks about, and a record of any call it ran without asking." },
      { text: "Your models: every cloud provider is an app you add or remove. Core ships only a local one and a small offline fallback." }
    ],
    docs: { href: "/docs/architecture/provider-boundary", label: "How agents and providers plug in" }
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Product · PersonalClaw"
  description="Explore PersonalClaw's agentic chat, autonomous goal loops, memory, knowledge, automation, and agent runtimes."
>
  <section class="tour" id="tour" aria-labelledby="product-title" data-tour data-param={CHAPTER_PARAM}>
    <div class="shell tour-grid">
      <div class="tour-copy">
        <h1 class="tour-title" id="product-title">Watch it do the work.</h1>
        <p class="tour-lede" data-support>
          One machine, in chapters: a chat, its approval, a goal loop, a knowledge search and what
          it made.
        </p>
        <p class="tour-note">{tourNote}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tour-frame" data-tour-frame>
        <video
          class="tour-video"
          width="1280"
          height="720"
          controls
          muted
          playsinline
          preload="none"
          poster="/media/personalclaw-tour-poster.webp"
          aria-label="The PersonalClaw tour"
        >
          <source src="/media/personalclaw-tour.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
          <track
            default
            kind="captions"
            srclang="en"
            label="English (described beats)"
            src="/media/personalclaw-tour.vtt"
          />
          <track kind="chapters" srclang="en" label="Chapters" src="/media/personalclaw-tour-chapters.vtt" />
        </video>
        <button type="button" class="tour-play" data-tour-play hidden>
          <CirclePlay size={28} aria-hidden="true" />
          <span data-tour-play-label>Play the tour</span>
        </button>
        <div class="tour-fallback" data-tour-fallback hidden>
          <img src="/media/personalclaw-tour-poster.webp" alt="" width="1280" height="720" />
          <div class="tour-outline">
            <p role="status">The tour could not play here. Its chapters, as text:</p>
            <ol>
              {chapters.map((chapter) => (
                <li>
                  <strong>{chapter.label}.</strong> {chapter.caption}
                </li>
              ))}
            </ol>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="tour-nav">
        <ol class="tour-chapters" aria-label="Tour chapters">
          {chapters.map((chapter, index) => (
            <li>
              <button
                type="button"
                class="chapter"
                data-chapter={chapter.id}
                data-start={chapter.start}
                aria-pressed={index === 0 ? "true" : "false"}
              >
                <span class="chapter-label">{chapter.label}</span>
                <span class="chapter-time">{clock(chapter.start)}</span>
              </button>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {capabilities.map((capability) => (
    <section
      class="capability section-rule"
      id={capability.id}
      aria-labelledby={`${capability.id}-title`}
      data-capability={capability.id}
    >
      <div class="shell capability-grid">
        <figure
          class="capability-media"
          style={`aspect-ratio: ${capability.capture.width} / ${capability.capture.height}`}
          data-media
        >
          <Image
            src={capability.capture}
            alt={capability.alt}
            widths={[640, 960, 1280]}
            sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 58vw"
            format="webp"
            quality={86}
          />
        </figure>
        <div class="capability-copy">
          <h2 class="capability-title" id={`${capability.id}-title`}>{capability.title}</h2>
          <p class="capability-problem">{capability.problem}</p>
          <h3 class="controls-title">What you control</h3>
          <ul class="controls">
            {capability.controls.map((control) => (
              <li>
                {control.text}
                {control.cite && (
                  <a class="text-link control-cite" href={control.cite.href}>{control.cite.label}</a>
                )}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <a class="text-link docs-link" href={capability.docs.href}>{capability.docs.label}</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <section class="product-cta" aria-labelledby="cta-title">
    <div class="narrow">
      <h2 class="section-title" id="cta-title">Run it on your machine.</h2>
      <p>
        The gateway, the dashboard and everything they keep stay on your machine. No
        analytics, no crash reporting and no usage reporting; by default it does ask GitHub
        for a newer release when it starts and on an interval, and one setting turns that off.
      </p>
      <p class="cta-source">
        <a class="cta-link" href="/compare#telemetry">What it sends, and what it does not</a>
      </p>
      <div class="cta-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary cta-primary" href={GITHUB_URL} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
          Get PersonalClaw
        </a>
        <a class="cta-link" href="/security">Read the security model</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  // The tour (#90, #96). Its chapter is kept in the URL (`?chapter=`) with `replaceState`, so a
  // reload, Back to the page and a copied link come back to it, and a choice adds no history entry.
  //
  // Nothing loads before a visitor asks. Choosing a chapter, or the tour's own play control, is
  // that ask: the tour fetches its file once, whole, then plays and seeks from memory, so a seek
  // never cancels a request in flight (tests/browser/support.ts fails on any failed request). A
  // chapter restored from the URL is only selected, and the first play starts there. Until then the
  // video has no source and no native controls, so nothing but these controls can start it; with no
  // script it keeps both and plays as a plain video.
  //
  // It pauses when it is scrolled out of view or its page is hidden, and never resumes on its own.
  // If its file cannot be fetched, or this browser cannot play it, the frame shows the poster, one
  // line saying so, and the chapters as text; nothing retries.
  const tour = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-tour]");
  const frame = tour?.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-tour-frame]");
  const video = frame?.querySelector("video");
  const source = video?.querySelector("source");
  const playButton = frame?.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-tour-play]");
  const fallback = frame?.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-tour-fallback]");
  if (tour && frame && video && source && playButton && fallback) {
    const param = tour.dataset.param ?? "chapter";
    const buttons = [...tour.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>("[data-chapter]")];
    const file = source.src;
    let loading: Promise<void> | null = null;
    let failed = false;

    const select = (id: string | null) => {
      const chosen = buttons.find((b) => b.dataset.chapter === id) ?? buttons[0];
      for (const b of buttons) b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(b === chosen));
      return chosen;
    };
    const selected = () => buttons.find((b) => b.getAttribute("aria-pressed") === "true") ?? buttons[0];
    const followUrl = () => select(new URLSearchParams(window.location.search).get(param));

    const fail = () => {
      failed = true;
      frame.dataset.failed = "";
      video.hidden = true;
      playButton.hidden = true;
      fallback.hidden = false;
    };

    // Detached before anything could ask for it: with preload="none" no request has been made.
    source.remove();
    video.controls = false;
    if (video.canPlayType("video/mp4") === "") fail();
    else playButton.hidden = false;

    const label = playButton.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-tour-play-label]");
    // While the file comes, the control says so and takes no second press. Once it is here the
    // video's own controls take over, and focus that was on this control moves to them.
    const load = () =>
      (loading ??= (() => {
        // aria-disabled, not disabled: a disabled button drops the focus it holds.
        playButton.setAttribute("aria-disabled", "true");
        playButton.setAttribute("aria-busy", "true");
        if (label) label.textContent = "Loading the tour…";
        return fetch(file)
          .then((response) => {
            if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`the tour answered ${response.status}`);
            return response.blob();
          })
          .then((blob) => {
            const focused = document.activeElement === playButton;
            video.src = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
            video.controls = true;
            playButton.hidden = true;
            playButton.removeAttribute("aria-busy");
            if (focused) video.focus({ preventScroll: true });
          });
      })());

    const play = async (from: HTMLButtonElement) => {
      if (failed) return;
      try {
        await load();
        video.currentTime = Number(from.dataset.start ?? 0);
        await video.play();
      } catch {
        fail();
      }
    };

    followUrl();
    window.addEventListener("pageshow", followUrl);
    video.addEventListener("error", fail);

    for (const button of buttons) {
      button.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const chosen = select(button.dataset.chapter ?? null);
        const url = new URL(window.location.href);
        if (chosen === buttons[0]) url.searchParams.delete(param);
        else url.searchParams.set(param, chosen.dataset.chapter ?? "");
        window.history.replaceState(window.history.state, "", url);
        void play(chosen);
      });
    }
    playButton.addEventListener("click", () => void play(selected()));

    new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
      if (entries.some((entry) => !entry.isIntersecting) && !video.paused) video.pause();
    }).observe(video);
    document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", () => {
      if (document.visibilityState === "hidden" && !video.paused) video.pause();
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  .tour {
    padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 5rem) clamp(4rem, 8vw, 7rem);
    background: var(--theater);
  }

  .tour-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(12, minmax(0, 1fr));
    grid-template-rows: auto 1fr;
    gap: 0 clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 3rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .tour-copy,
  .tour-nav {
    grid-column: 1 / span 4;
  }

  .tour-frame {
    position: relative;
    grid-column: 5 / -1;
    grid-row: 1 / span 2;
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 1px solid var(--line-strong);
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: var(--surface);
    box-shadow: 0 40px 100px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.48);
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
  }

  .tour-video {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
  }

  .tour-video[hidden],
  .tour-play[hidden] {
    display: none;
  }

  /* The tour's own play control, over the poster until its file is here. */
  .tour-play {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    display: inline-flex;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    align-items: center;
    /* Transparent, so forced colors draw the control's edge once its fill is gone. */
    border: 2px solid transparent;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--coral);
    color: var(--night);
    font: inherit;
    font-weight: var(--fw-label);
    white-space: nowrap;
    cursor: pointer;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  }

  .tour-play[aria-busy="true"] {
    cursor: progress;
  }

  /* With no video, the frame grows to hold the poster and the chapters as text. */
  .tour-frame[data-failed] {
    aspect-ratio: auto;
  }

  .tour-fallback img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  .tour-outline {
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem 1.5rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
  }

  .tour-outline p {
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    color: var(--ink);
    font-weight: var(--fw-label);
  }

  .tour-outline ol {
    display: grid;
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .tour-outline strong {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .tour-title {
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
    font-size: var(--fs-heading);
    font-weight: var(--fw-heading);
    line-height: var(--lh-heading);
  }

  .tour-lede {
    max-width: 68ch;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .tour-note {
    max-width: 68ch;
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
  }

  .tour-chapters {
    display: grid;
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    padding: 0;
    gap: 0.25rem;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .chapter {
    display: flex;
    width: 100%;
    min-height: 44px;
    padding: 0.55rem 1rem;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--fs-label);
